// what a command's prologue does about SIGPIPE, and where a failed write to stdout goes. on Darwin and
// Linux the signal is ignored before the first statement, and a failed `echo` asks
// `__eco_stdio_write_failed`. Windows has no SIGPIPE, so main does neither. `std::env`'s trap still
// names libc `signal` in its own functions, which is why the Windows CHECK-NOT is scoped to main

echo 1;
